Step 1: Restrict Access to the Admin Page
Option A: IP Whitelisting (Best for Private Networks)

Allow access only from trusted IPs:

Nginx Example:

location /admin/ {
    allow 192.168.1.0/24;
    deny all;
}

Apache Example:

<Directory "/var/www/html/admin">
    Require ip 192.168.1.0/24
</Directory>

Option B: Hide Admin Path (Security through Obscurity + Real Security)

Change the default admin URL:

Django:

# urls.py
urlpatterns = [
    path('my-secret-admin/', admin.site.urls),
]

Flask-Admin:

admin = Admin(app, url='/my-secret-admin')

Laravel:

Use a custom route or admin middleware group.
✅ Step 2: Protect Against Brute-Force Login Attempts
Option A: Rate Limiting (Best Practice)
Middleware / Package-Based:

    Django: Use django-axes

pip install django-axes

In settings.py:

INSTALLED_APPS += ['axes']
MIDDLEWARE += ['axes.middleware.AxesMiddleware']

Then run:

python manage.py migrate

It will automatically block failed login attempts based on IP or username.

    Flask: Use Flask-Limiter

from flask_limiter import Limiter

limiter = Limiter(app, default_limits=["5 per minute"])

    Laravel: Use built-in throttling:

// In routes/web.php
Route::post('/login', 'Auth\LoginController@login')->middleware('throttle:5,1');

Option B: CAPTCHA After Failed Attempts

Add a CAPTCHA (like Google reCAPTCHA) after 3-5 failed login attempts.

Most frameworks support it via middleware or form plugins.
✅ Step 3: Add 2FA (Optional but Strongly Recommended)

Enable Two-Factor Authentication (2FA) for your admin login using:

    Django: django-two-factor-auth

    Laravel: Use built-in 2FA via Laravel Fortify or Jetstream

    Flask: Use Flask-Security or Flask-User with 2FA support

✅ Step 4: Monitor Login Attempts

Log and alert on repeated failed logins:

    Use tools like Fail2Ban to automatically block IPs

    Send alerts on suspicious logins
